---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import FluidsSim from '../../simulators/fluids/FluidsSim.astro';
import { fluidsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/fluids';

const toc = [
  { id: 'fluids', label: 'What is a fluid?' },
  { id: 'density', label: 'Density' },
  { id: 'pressure', label: 'Pressure in a fluid' },
  { id: 'gauge', label: 'Gauge and absolute pressure' },
  { id: 'buoyancy', label: 'Where buoyancy comes from' },
  { id: 'archimedes', label: 'Archimedes’ principle' },
  { id: 'float-sink', label: 'Float, sink or hover?' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model assumes' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Ling, S. J., Sanny, J., & Moebs, W. (2016). University Physics Volume 1, Sections 14.1–14.4: Fluids, Density, Pressure, Pascal’s Principle and Archimedes’ Principle. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-1-fluids-density-and-pressure' },
  { text: 'Urone, P. P., & Hinrichs, R. (2020). College Physics 2e, Chapter 11: Fluid Statics. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/11-introduction-to-fluid-statics' },
  { text: 'Halliday, D., Resnick, R., & Walker, J. (2018). Fundamentals of Physics (11th ed.), Chapter 14: Fluids. Wiley.' },
  { text: 'White, F. M. (2016). Fluid Mechanics (8th ed.), Chapter 2: Pressure Distribution in a Fluid. McGraw-Hill.' },
  { text: 'Lide, D. R. (Ed.). CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics: densities of common liquids and solids. CRC Press.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="physics"
  topic="fluids-buoyancy"
  description="Interactive buoyancy and fluid pressure simulator: drop objects into a tank, change the fluid and the objects’ density, measure pressure with depth, and see Archimedes’ principle and why things float, sink or hover."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={fluidsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A steel ship weighing thousands of tonnes floats, while a small pebble sinks. Your ears hurt at the bottom of a swimming
      pool. A submarine can hover in mid-water. All of these come from one idea: pressure in a fluid grows with depth. Drop
      objects into the tank, change the fluid, and measure the pressure for yourself.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <FluidsSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Drag the <strong>objects</strong> A, B and C in and out of the tank, or onto the shelf. Tap one to select it.</li>
      <li>Change the selected object’s <strong>shape</strong>, <strong>volume</strong>, <strong>density</strong> or <strong>mass</strong>, or pick a material. Press <strong>Match the fluid</strong> to make it neutrally buoyant.</li>
      <li>Change the <strong>fluid</strong> with the slider or the presets, and the fluid level. Watch objects rise or sink.</li>
      <li>Drag the yellow <strong>pressure probe</strong> to different depths. Switch between gauge and absolute pressure, and save fluids on the graph to compare them.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Hold the selected object</strong> and drag it deeper: the Archimedes panel shows the displaced fluid and the force panel shows the hand force needed.</li>
      <li>Tap anything in the picture for an explanation. <strong>Reset</strong> restores every setting.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="fluids">What is a fluid?</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>fluid</strong> is a substance that flows: liquids and gases. Its particles can move past each other, so a
    fluid takes the shape of its container. In this simulator the fluid is at rest, so we can use <strong>hydrostatics</strong>,
    the physics of fluids that are not moving.
  </p>

  <h2 id="density">Density</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Density ρ = m / V</strong> (mass per unit volume) tells you how tightly mass is packed. It is measured in
    kg/m³. Fresh water has a density of about 1000 kg/m³ (1 kg per litre), sea water about 1025 kg/m³, vegetable oil about
    920 kg/m³ and honey about 1420 kg/m³. For an object made of several materials, or with air inside, what matters is its
    <strong>average density</strong>: its total mass divided by its total volume.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pressure">Pressure in a fluid</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Pressure</strong> is force per unit area, p = F / A, measured in pascals (1 Pa = 1 N/m²). A fluid at rest
    pushes on every surface it touches, at right angles to that surface. The deeper you go, the more fluid lies above you,
    so the pressure rises:
  </p>
  <p><strong>p = p₀ + ρ g h</strong></p>
  <p>
    Here p₀ is the pressure at the surface (the atmosphere, about 101.3 kPa), ρ is the fluid’s density, g ≈ 9.81 m/s² and h
    is the depth below the surface. Pressure depends only on depth and density, not on the shape or width of the container,
    and at a given depth it pushes equally in all directions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gauge">Gauge and absolute pressure</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Formula</th><th>At 0.5 m in water</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Absolute</strong></td><td>p = p₀ + ρgh</td><td>≈ 106.2 kPa</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gauge</strong></td><td>p_g = ρgh</td><td>≈ 4.9 kPa</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Absolute pressure is measured from a perfect vacuum. Gauge pressure is the extra pressure above the atmosphere, which is
    what a tyre gauge reads. Because the atmosphere pushes equally on all sides of a submerged object and on both sides of
    the tank walls, its effect cancels, so the arrows in the simulator show gauge pressure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="buoyancy">Where buoyancy comes from</h2>
  <p>
    The bottom of a submerged object is deeper than its top, so the fluid pushes harder on the bottom than on the top. The
    sideways pushes cancel out. What is left is a net upward force: the <strong>buoyant force</strong>, sometimes called
    upthrust. You can see this in the pressure arrows on the objects: longer at the bottom, shorter at the top.
  </p>

  <h2 id="archimedes">Archimedes’ principle</h2>
  <p>
    Adding up all those pushes gives a remarkably simple result, first described by Archimedes over 2,200 years ago: the
    buoyant force equals the <strong>weight of the fluid displaced</strong> by the object.
  </p>
  <p><strong>B = ρ<sub>fluid</sub> g V<sub>displaced</sub></strong></p>
  <p>
    V<sub>displaced</sub> is the volume of the part of the object below the surface. A fully submerged 1 L object in water
    displaces 1 L of water, weighing 9.81 N, so B = 9.81 N, whether it is a cube, a sphere or a cylinder, and whether it is
    made of cork or of steel. The displaced fluid also raises the fluid level in the tank.
  </p>

  <h2 id="float-sink">Float, sink or hover?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ρ_object &lt; ρ_fluid: it floats.</strong> It rises until it displaces its own weight of fluid. The fraction below the surface is ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid.</li>
    <li><strong>ρ_object &gt; ρ_fluid: it sinks.</strong> Even fully submerged, B &lt; W. On the bottom it feels lighter: its apparent weight is W − B.</li>
    <li><strong>ρ_object = ρ_fluid: it is neutrally buoyant</strong> and stays at whatever depth it is placed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A steel ship floats because its hull encloses a large volume of air, so its <em>average</em> density is less than water’s.
    The same object can float in one fluid and sink in another: an egg sinks in fresh water but floats in very salty water.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ships and boats</strong> float by displacing their own weight of water; the waterline (Plimsoll) marks show how low they may safely ride.</li>
    <li><strong>Submarines and fish</strong> change their average density with ballast tanks or a swim bladder to rise, sink or hover.</li>
    <li><strong>Hot-air balloons</strong> float in the “fluid” of the atmosphere, because hot air is less dense than the cold air around it.</li>
    <li><strong>Dams</strong> are thicker at the bottom, where the water pressure is greatest; <strong>divers</strong> must equalise the pressure in their ears as they descend.</li>
    <li><strong>Hydrometers</strong> measure the density of liquids, such as the sugar content of juice or the charge of a battery, by how deep they float.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Heavy things sink and light things float.”</strong> What matters is density compared with the fluid, not weight.</li>
    <li><strong>“The buoyant force always equals the object’s weight.”</strong> Only for a floating object; a sinking object has B &lt; W.</li>
    <li><strong>“Deeper objects feel more buoyant force.”</strong> Once fully submerged, B is the same at any depth, because the displaced volume does not change.</li>
    <li><strong>“Pressure in a fluid only pushes down.”</strong> It pushes in all directions, at right angles to every surface.</li>
    <li><strong>“Shape decides whether something floats.”</strong> Shape matters only because it changes how much water the object can displace (like a hollow hull).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model assumes</h2>
  <p>
    The fluid is at rest and incompressible, with the same density everywhere, so the simple hydrostatic law p = p₀ + ρgh
    applies; g = 9.81 m/s² and the atmosphere is 101.3 kPa. The objects are solid and uniform, move only up and down, and do
    not tip, so a cube stays upright. While settling they feel a simple drag that only affects how they move, not where they
    come to rest. Surface tension and the motion of the fluid itself are ignored. Objects in the tank are kept from
    overlapping by moving them sideways. The tank is 60 cm wide and 30 cm from front to back, which sets how much the level
    rises.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pressure in a fluid at rest grows with depth: p = p₀ + ρgh; gauge pressure leaves out the atmosphere.</li>
    <li>The extra push on an object’s lower surface produces the buoyant force.</li>
    <li>Archimedes’ principle: B equals the weight of the fluid displaced, B = ρ_fluid g V_displaced.</li>
    <li>Objects float, sink or hover depending on their average density compared with the fluid’s.</li>
    <li>A floating object has ρ_object/ρ_fluid of its volume below the surface.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
